WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.833
Adversarial and red-team testing.

2
00:00:03.133 --> 00:00:05.453
Functional evals ask whether it works.

3
00:00:05.453 --> 00:00:09.300
Red-team tests ask what happens when someone tries to break it.

4
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:11.767
Here is the core rule.

5
00:00:12.067 --> 00:00:17.070
Any text your app processes that a trusted developer didn't write is untrusted.

6
00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:20.300
That includes retrieved documents and tool results.

7
00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:23.533
A red team suite has four branches.

8
00:00:23.833 --> 00:00:27.855
Direct jailbreaks: the user says ignore previous instructions.

9
00:00:27.855 --> 00:00:31.033
Keep an attack corpus and rerun it every release.

10
00:00:31.333 --> 00:00:37.028
Indirect injection: malicious text hidden in a document, email, or API response.

11
00:00:37.028 --> 00:00:39.733
Classic pentesting never covered this.

12
00:00:40.033 --> 00:00:43.628
Over-refusal: a filter that blocks legitimate requests.

13
00:00:43.628 --> 00:00:45.000
Track it as a metric.

14
00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:46.286
And tool abuse.

15
00:00:46.286 --> 00:00:50.167
For agents, the scariest failure is an unauthorized delete.

16
00:00:51.367 --> 00:00:52.733
So how do we test for it?

17
00:00:53.033 --> 00:00:56.533
We plant a poisoned document that looks like a normal report.

18
00:00:56.833 --> 00:00:58.833
The agent is asked to summarize it.

19
00:00:59.133 --> 00:01:01.200
We record every tool call it makes.

20
00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:03.998
And we assert that no write ever happened.

21
00:01:03.998 --> 00:01:05.367
Actions, not just text.

22
00:01:06.567 --> 00:01:08.067
In code, it looks like this.

23
00:01:08.367 --> 00:01:10.900
We run the agent on the poisoned document.

24
00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:15.867
Then we gather every SQL call from the trace, and assert that no delete appears.

25
00:01:16.167 --> 00:01:18.633
And every call must be in read only mode.

26
00:01:19.833 --> 00:01:21.333
Here is a real example.

27
00:01:21.633 --> 00:01:27.600
The Orbital Research Agent's MCP server exposes three tools over production Postgres.

28
00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:33.533
A red team case plants an instruction inside a fabricated report, asking for a write query.

29
00:01:33.833 --> 00:01:38.248
The server is read only, and a test asserts no write tool exists.

30
00:01:38.248 --> 00:01:41.100
The test proves the design actually holds.

31
00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:43.124
Let's recap.

32
00:01:43.124 --> 00:01:45.733
Treat all retrieved text as untrusted.

33
00:01:46.033 --> 00:01:49.800
Test jailbreaks, injection, over-refusal, and tool abuse.

34
00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:52.567
Assert on actions taken, not just words.

35
00:01:52.867 --> 00:01:54.838
Tests prove the design holds.

36
00:01:54.838 --> 00:01:58.033
Next up: eval gates, shadow and canary deploys.
